# Copy this file to ".env" and adjust for your setup, then: docker compose up -d
#
# ── Local testing (default) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Works out of the box on the machine running Docker. Open http://localhost:8080
ORIGIN=http://localhost:8080
WEB_PORT=8080

# ── Real deployment behind your own HTTPS domain ───────────────────────────
# Put Forvia behind a reverse proxy / tunnel that terminates TLS for your
# domain, then set:
#
#   ORIGIN=https://gym.example.com
#   WEB_PORT=8080
#
# (Point your proxy at the web container's WEB_PORT. See docs/SELF_HOSTING.md.)

# ── Admin dashboard & invite-only signup (optional) ────────────────────────
# Both off by default — a fresh instance has open signup and no admin.
#
# Make yourself an admin: register your own account first, find your user id
# with `docker compose exec db psql -U forvia -c "select id, name from kv_users"`
# (or just its "data" column — see api/db.js), then list it here (comma-separated
# for several admins). Admins get an "Admin dashboard" link in Settings to see
# users, disable accounts, generate invite codes and view workout history.
#
#   ADMIN_UIDS=youruserid,anotheradmin
#
# Require an invite code to create new profiles (existing accounts keep working;
# generate the codes from the admin dashboard):
#
#   INVITE_ONLY=1
#
# Remove the "Continue without account" button, so a profile is the only way in.
# Guest mode keeps everything in the browser and never touches this server, but
# on an instance meant for a known set of people it is still the wrong front
# door. Anyone already inside as a guest is signed out on their next visit; the
# data they made stays in that browser. Defaults to on (guests allowed):
#
#   ALLOW_GUEST=0

# ── Activity log (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The admin dashboard shows an activity log: who signed in, who tried and
# failed, and every admin action (disabling an account, creating or revoking an
# invite code). It lives in the audit_log table in Postgres (see api/db.js) —
# query it directly with `docker compose exec db psql -U forvia -c "select data from audit_log order by id"`.
#
# It is ON by default. It records strictly less than this instance already
# holds — every profile and every workout is in the same Postgres database,
# readable by any admin — and a log that ships switched off tells you
# nothing on the day you need it. Turn it off completely with:
#
#   AUDIT_LOG=0
#
# Retention is a cap, not an archive. Whichever limit is reached first wins;
# set either to 0 to disable that limit:
#
#   AUDIT_MAX=5000            # events kept
#   AUDIT_DAYS=90             # days kept
#
# IP addresses are the one thing that is OFF unless you ask, because they are
# the only field here that says where somebody physically is. They are also the
# only way to tell one failed sign-in from twenty. Three settings:
#
#   AUDIT_IP=off              # default — no addresses at all
#   AUDIT_IP=net              # network only: 203.0.113.0/24, 2001:db8:1::/48
#   AUDIT_IP=full             # the whole address
#
# The address is read from CF-Connecting-IP, then the first entry of
# X-Forwarded-For, then X-Real-IP. The web container sets the last two, so the
# bundled stack is correct as-is. Cloudflare is first in that list on purpose: a
# Cloudflare tunnel does not put the visitor in X-Forwarded-For at all, so that
# header would otherwise record the tunnel itself and look perfectly plausible.
# Whatever you run in front has to overwrite these headers rather than pass a
# client-supplied one through — they are only as trustworthy as your proxy.

# ── Session length (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# How long a sign-in lasts, in days. Default 90 — long enough that people who
# train regularly never see a login screen, short enough that a stolen cookie
# doesn't stay good forever. Lower it for an instance on the open internet,
# raise it for a private one. Only affects new sign-ins; cookies already handed
# out keep the lifetime they were issued with. Users can end every session on
# every device themselves with "Sign out everywhere" in Settings.
#
#   SESSION_DAYS=90

# ── Fitting into an existing stack (optional) ──────────────────────────────
# The defaults assume Forvia is on its own: an API service called `api` on
# port 3000, a forvia-core service called `core-api` also on port 3000, and
# nginx listening on 80 inside the web container. If you are merging this into
# a compose file that already has services by those names, or you front the
# web container with your own reverse proxy, move them here. The web image
# renders its nginx config at start-up, so these apply to a prebuilt image and
# need no rebuild — they must name services the web container can reach on
# your network:
#
#   NGINX_PORT=80
#   BACKEND=api
#   PORT=3000
#   CORE_BACKEND=core-api
#   CORE_PORT=3000

# ── forvia-core (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Forvia's own auth/sessions/account/data-sync/base-admin half lives in its own
# service and repo now — github.com/Nebula-Syst/forvia-core (still AGPL; see
# that repo's own NOTICE.md for why). `docker compose up` brings it up
# alongside `api`; the two talk to each other over a small internal API that
# is never exposed publicly, proven by a shared secret you set once here (any
# long random string — `openssl rand -hex 32` works):
#
#   INTERNAL_SECRET=pick-something-long-and-random

# ── Database (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Forvia stores everything in PostgreSQL — a `db` container comes bundled in
# docker-compose.yml, nothing to install separately. Only its password is worth
# setting yourself instead of the default:
#
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pick-something-random
#
# Pointing at a Postgres you already run elsewhere instead of the bundled
# container: drop the `db` service from docker-compose.yml and set
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host:5432/dbname yourself.

# ── Notifications (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Push works out of the box — nothing to set. VAPID keys are generated on first
# run and saved to ./data/vapid.json. Each user's browser reports its own
# timezone automatically, so the workout-day reminder fires at their local
# time regardless of where the server runs or where they're travelling.
#
# Push services want a contact address for whoever operates the server, in case
# they need to get in touch about your pushes. Defaults to your ORIGIN, which is
# fine; set a mailto: if you would rather they mailed you:
#
#   VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ── Box address search (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A coach's own location (their profile, the marketplace) always uses the free,
# keyless place search — OpenStreetMap data via Komoot's Photon — since "what
# city/town" is all it needs. A box's exact street address is pickier, and has
# three modes:
#
#   BOX_LOCATION_MODE=off      Plain manual text — whatever the coach types is
#                               saved as-is, no geocoding at all. No lat/lon (so
#                               no map/distance for a box, just the address text
#                               as typed). Right for an instance that doesn't
#                               want any third-party lookup for this field, or
#                               hasn't set one up yet.
#   BOX_LOCATION_MODE=search   Default. The same free Photon/OSM search as a
#                               coach's own location. Has real gaps for exact
#                               house numbers on ordinary residential streets,
#                               especially outside big cities: a house Google
#                               Maps resolves fine can come back with nothing
#                               from Photon because OSM never had it mapped.
#   BOX_LOCATION_MODE=precise  Also merges in Google's Geocoding API (below) —
#                               only for this one field, still Photon-only for a
#                               coach's own location either way. Silently
#                               behaves like `search` until GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY
#                               is actually set, so it's safe to set ahead of
#                               getting a key.
#
#   BOX_LOCATION_MODE=off
#
# Google's Geocoding API is metered, paid usage on your own Google Cloud billing
# account (needs billing enabled to get a key at all); it currently includes
# 10,000 free requests/month, then charges per request above that (check
# https://developers.google.com/maps/billing-and-pricing/pricing for current
# numbers — Google changes these). A small self-hosted instance — a handful of
# coaches occasionally requesting a box — will likely never leave the free tier.
#
#   GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=your-key-with-the-geocoding-api-enabled
